WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Part 4. You will hear a student on a fashion design course giving a talk about the history of pockets.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.000
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:50.000
You will hear a student on a fashion design course giving a talk about the history of pockets.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Good morning. Now we've been asked to choose an aspect of European clothing or fashion and to talk about its development over time.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:27.000
I decided to focus on a rather small area of clothing and that's pockets.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.000
I chose pockets for two reasons really.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:39.000
We all have them in jeans, jackets, coats, for example, and even though we often carry bags or briefcases as well,

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:47.000
nothing is quite as convenient as being able to pop your phone or credit card into your pocket.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:57.000
Yet, I suspect that other than that, people don't really think about pockets too much and they're rather overlooked as a fashion item.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:04.000
It's certainly very interesting to go back in time and see how pockets developed for men and women.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:12.000
In the 18th century, fashions were quite different from the way they are now and pockets were too.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:20.000
If we think about male fashion first, that was the time when suits became popular.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.000
Trousers were knee-length only and referred to as breeches.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.000
The waistcoats were short and the jackets were long.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:39.000
But all three garments were lined with material and pockets were sewn into this cloth by whichever tailor the customer used.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:49.000
The wearer could then carry small objects such as pencils or coins on their person and reach them through a gap in the lining.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Coat pockets became increasingly decorative on the outside for men who wanted to look stylish.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:04.000
But they were often larger but plainer if the wearer was someone with a profession who needed to carry medical instruments,

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:12.000
a doctor or physician, for example.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.000
The development of women's pockets was a little different.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:23.000
For one thing, they weren't nearly as visible or as easy to reach as men's.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:34.000
In the 18th and 19th centuries, women carried numerous possessions on their person and some of these could be worth a lot of money.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:43.000
Women were more vulnerable to theft and wealthy women, in particular, worried constantly about pickpockets.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:50.000
So, what they did was to have a pair of pockets made that were tied together with string.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:56.000
The pockets were made of fabric, which might be recycled cloth if the wearer had little money,

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:04.000
or something more expensive, such as linen, sometimes featuring very delicate embroidery.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:10.000
Women tied the pockets around their waist so that they hung beneath their clothes.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:21.000
Remember, skirts were long then and there was plenty of room to hide a whole range of small possessions between the layers of petticoats that were commonly worn.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:29.000
They would have an opening in the folds of their skirts through which they could reach whatever they needed, like their perfume.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:38.000
Working women, of course, also needed to carry around items that they might use for whatever job or trade they were involved in.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:44.000
But their pairs of pockets still remained on the inside of their clothing.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:50.000
They just got bigger or longer, sometimes reaching down to their knees.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:05:01.000
So, the tie-on pockets went well into the 19th century and only changed when fashion altered towards the end of that period.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:09.000
That's when dresses became tighter and less bulky, and the pairs of pockets became very noticeable.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.000
They stood out too much and detracted from the woman's image.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:25.000
Women who had been used to carrying around a range of personal possessions, and still wanted to, needed somewhere to carry these items about their person.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:31.000
That was when small bags, or pouches as they were known, came into fashion.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:42.000
And, of course, they inevitably led onto the handbag of more modern times, particularly when fashion removed pockets altogether.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:05.000
Thank you for watching.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:50.000
.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.000
That is the end of the listening test.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:07:01.000
